What it is. The app checks that you are logged in, but not what you may touch. Users read other users' data, change IDs in URLs, or call admin endpoints. The classic form is the IDOR (insecure direct object reference): /invoices/2 works for anyone who guesses 2.
# a01_idor.py — Broken Access Control: insecure direct object reference
from fastapi import FastAPI, Header, HTTPException
app = FastAPI()
INVOICES = {1: {"owner": "ada", "total": 120}, 2: {"owner": "bob", "total": 999}}
TOKENS = {"token-ada": "ada", "token-bob": "bob"} # stand-in for a real login
def user_from(token: str) -> str:
if token not in TOKENS:
raise HTTPException(401, "Not logged in")
return TOKENS[token]
@app.get("/v1/invoices/{inv_id}") # VULNERABLE: checks login, not ownership
def invoice_v1(inv_id: int, x_token: str = Header()):
user_from(x_token)
if inv_id not in INVOICES:
raise HTTPException(404)
return INVOICES[inv_id]
@app.get("/v2/invoices/{inv_id}") # FIXED: the record must belong to the caller
def invoice_v2(inv_id: int, x_token: str = Header()):
user = user_from(x_token)
inv = INVOICES.get(inv_id)
if inv is None or inv["owner"] != user:
raise HTTPException(404) # same answer for "missing" and "not yours"
return inv
curl -H "X-Token: token-ada" localhost:8000/v1/invoices/2 # {"owner":"bob","total":999} leak
curl -H "X-Token: token-ada" localhost:8000/v2/invoices/2 # {"detail":"Not Found"}
The fix. Deny by default. Check ownership on the server for every record, ideally in the query itself (WHERE id = ? AND owner = ?). Put the check in one shared place so no route forgets it. Random IDs (UUIDs) make guessing harder but are not access control.

Server-Side Request Forgery happens when your server fetches a URL that the user chose. The attacker makes your server call places only it can reach: localhost admin pages, internal services, or the cloud metadata address 169.254.169.254 that can hand out credentials (on AWS, requiring IMDSv2 makes this harder).
# a01_ssrf.py — Server-Side Request Forgery (part of A01 since 2025)
import ipaddress, socket, urllib.request
from urllib.parse import urlparse
from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException
app = FastAPI()
@app.get("/v1/preview") # VULNERABLE: fetches any URL it is given
def preview_v1(url: str):
with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=3) as r:
return {"status": r.status, "start": r.read(100).decode("utf-8", "replace")}
ALLOWED_HOSTS = {"example.com", "www.example.com"}
class NoRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
def redirect_request(self, *args, **kwargs):
return None # a redirect could point inside
def resolves_to_public_ip(host: str) -> bool:
addrs = {info[4][0] for info in socket.getaddrinfo(host, None)}
return all(ipaddress.ip_address(a).is_global for a in addrs)
@app.get("/v2/preview") # FIXED: allowlist, public IPs only, no redirects
def preview_v2(url: str):
u = urlparse(url)
if u.scheme not in ("http", "https") or u.hostname not in ALLOWED_HOSTS:
raise HTTPException(400, "URL not allowed")
if not resolves_to_public_ip(u.hostname):
raise

allowlist, public IPs only, no redirects
def preview_v2(url: str):
u = urlparse(url)
if u.scheme not in ("http", "https") or u.hostname not in ALLOWED_HOSTS:
raise HTTPException(400, "URL not allowed")
if not resolves_to_public_ip(u.hostname):
raise HTTPException(400, "URL not allowed")
opener = urllib.request.build_opener(NoRedirect)
with opener.open(url, timeout=3) as r:
return {"status": r.status, "start": r.read(100).decode("utf-8", "replace")}
Run it and ask /v1/preview to fetch the app's own API docs: curl "localhost:8000/v1/preview?url=http://127.0.0.1:8000/openapi.json". The server happily reads an internal address. /v2/preview answers 400 for 127.0.0.1, 169.254.169.254 and file:///....
Watch out: the name can resolve to a public IP when you check it and a private one when you fetch it (DNS rebinding). Strong setups also block outbound traffic at the network level, so the app server simply cannot reach internal ranges.

XSS is injection into HTML. If user input reaches the page unescaped, its <script> runs in other users' browsers, with their session.
# a05_xss.py — Cross-site scripting and its fix
import html
from fastapi import FastAPI
from fastapi.responses import HTMLResponse
app = FastAPI()
CSP = "default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
@app.get("/v1/hello", response_class=HTMLResponse) # VULNERABLE
def hello_v1(name: str = "world"):
return f"<h1>Hello {name}</h1>"
@app.get("/v2/hello") # FIXED: escape + CSP
def hello_v2(name: str = "world"):
page = f"<h1>Hello {html.escape(name)}</h1>"
return HTMLResponse(page, headers={"Content-Security-Policy": CSP})
Open http://localhost:8000/v1/hello?name=<script>alert(1)</script> in a browser and the alert pops up. On /v2/hello the same input shows as text, because it arrives as &lt;script&gt;….
In the browser, the same rule applies to JavaScript:
const name = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get("name");
document.querySelector("#greet").innerHTML = `Hello ${name}`; // VULNERABLE
document.querySelector("#greet").textContent = `Hello ${name}`; // FIXED: always text
